//! check: run(exit=1, stdout="line: no input\nprompt: anonymous\nerror: eof")
//! phase: run
//! conforms: std.io, err.row, err.propagate, err.else
//!
//! The input side, at the only depth the rig can reach: `conform-run` has
//! no stdin injection at this pin, so standard input is EMPTY and every
//! read misses with `eof` (the hit path is proven upstream, in the
//! compiler's own io/fs tests; the flag std would need to prove it here is
//! filed as F-0046).
//!
//! What that still pins, and it is not nothing: `eof` is a ROW rather than
//! an empty string or a trap, so a caller distinguishes "the input ended"
//! from "the user typed nothing" — the distinction a sentinel would
//! destroy. `prompt` misses the same way after writing its prompt, and the
//! propagation out of `main` names the tag on stdout, which is this rig's
//! only witness of a tag's identity (F-0043).
//!
//! Note the prompt itself does NOT appear in stdout before the miss:
//! `prompt` writes with `write` (no newline) and the empty-stdin read
//! misses immediately, so the two writes are the two lines below and the
//! prompt text rides on the first of them. It is written into the
//! expectation deliberately — a reader who expected otherwise has learned
//! something true about prompts.

use std.io

fn main() -> !int {
    let line = io.input_line() else "no input"
    io.write_line("line: {line}")
    let name = io.prompt("") else "anonymous"
    io.write_line("prompt: {name}")
    let strict = io.input_line()?
    io.write_line("unreachable: {strict}")
    0
}
